  "notes": [
    "Criteria: C1 average unit cost, C2 annual dollar usage, C3 lead time. All three are treated as beneficial: a higher value moves an item towards class A (Section 3).",
    "The paper says the weights are \"equally distributed\" but prints none. The printed SP_i and SN_i are the plain sums of PDA and NDA, so the fixture uses weights of 1. Any equal weights give the same NSP, NSN and AS, because both sums are divided by their maximum; a golden test that normalises weights to 1/3 must divide its SP and SN by 3 before comparing them with this table.",
    "Table 3 lists the items by decreasing AS; tied printed scores (for example S8 and S5, both 0.60) are ordered by their unrounded values, which the fixture ranking reproduces.",
    "Several printed two-decimal values are one unit higher than the recomputed value rounded once (for example 0.7446 printed as 0.75). Every such case lies between x.xx45 and x.xx50, so the paper evidently rounded to three decimals first and then to two. They are listed as deviations."
  ]
